# Line endings, pinned, because a checkout that differs from the tree the tests
# were written against is a suite that passes for its author and fails for
# everybody else.
#
# The failure this prevents: with `core.autocrlf=true`, which is the Windows
# default and can come from the system config, every file git materialises gets
# CRLF, and vitest cannot load a `.mjs` with CRLF in it. The symptom is
# `SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token` with no file, no line and no stack.
# It is invisible to whoever wrote the file, because git does not rewrite a
# working file it has just read: only a fresh checkout gets the conversion, and
# a fresh checkout is exactly what CI does.
#
# `text=auto` keeps git's own binary detection, `eol=lf` says what the working
# tree gets. Nothing in the repository changes: the index has always held LF,
# because autocrlf normalises on the way in. This file only settles what comes
# back out.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The binaries this repo actually carries, named rather than left to detection,
# because a PNG that gets line-ending treatment is a corrupt PNG and the icons
# are the app's face.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.woff2 binary
